Here's a complete example of a URL builder class in Java:

```java
import java.net.URI;
import java.net.URISyntaxException;
import java.util.List;

public class UrlBuilder {
    private String scheme;
    private String netloc;
    private String path;
    private String query;

    public UrlBuilder() {}

    public void setScheme(String scheme) {
        this.scheme = scheme;
    }

    public void setNetloc(String netloc) {
        this.netloc = netloc;
    }

    public void setPath(String path) {
        this.path = path;
    }

    public void addQueryParameter(String key, String value) {
        if (query != null) {
            query += "&" + key + "=" + value;
        } else {
            query = key + "=" + value;
        }
    }

    public UrlBuilder withQuery(String query) {
        this.query = query;
        return this;
    }

    public String getScheme() {
        return scheme;
    }

    public String getNetloc() {
        return netloc;
    }

    public String getPath() {
        return path;
    }

    public String getQuery() {
        return query;
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return new URI(scheme, netloc, path, query).toString();
    }
}
```

And here's an example of how you could use it:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        UrlBuilder builder = new UrlBuilder()
                .withQuery("key=value")
                .setPath("/path")
                .addQueryParameter("foo", "bar")
                .addQueryParameter("baz", "qux");

        System.out.println(builder.toString()); // prints "https://example.com/path?key=value&foo=bar&baz=qux"
    }
}
```

In this example, the `UrlBuilder` class provides a fluent API for building URLs. You can chain method calls together to set each component of the URL.

Please note that this is just one way to implement a URL builder class in Java, and there are many variations and edge cases depending on your specific requirements.
